{
  "assessment": "The source provides a quantitative analysis of chaotic fluctuations in transmon qubit gates, focusing on the KL divergence of eigenphase spacings and curvature statistics. The best quantitative cluster is the curvature power-law decay for the BGATE, with two regimes (Poisson and GUE) distinguished by the time interval. The controls are time t and the curvature k, and the readout is the probability distribution P(k). The source reports fitted exponents beta=2 and beta=4 for the two regimes, with a normalization constant. The case is authoring-ready because it has numeric controls, a numeric readout, a quantitative mechanism, and an objective response within an explicit domain.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Daniel Basilewitsch, Chaotic Fluctuations in a Universal Set of Transmon Qubit Gates, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/8nch-ld18",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/8nch-ld18",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2311.14592v2"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper analyzes signatures of non-integrability and chaos in a universal set of transmon qubit gates (Hadamard, local phase, and BGATE) implemented via optimal control on two transmons coupled to a cavity. It uses spectral statistics (KL divergence of eigenphase spacings) and curvature statistics to identify chaotic fluctuations, and studies robustness of the fast BGATE to parameter deviations.",
